---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

// docs-refactor Phase 3 (#998): the "44/44 FieldHandle methods — full parity" line this page used to
// carry was FALSE and had been for months — `pnpm gen:parity-matrix` reads the ports directly and has
// never agreed with it. The counts below are read out of that generated artifact so the claim cannot
// drift again. Do not retype a number here.
import parityData from '../../../../../data/parity-matrix.json';
const handleDim = parityData.dimensions.find((d: { id: string }) => d.id === 'field-handle-methods')!;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Fundamental — Implementations"
  description="One field engine, three surfaces: the web DOM binding, native Swift and Kotlin ports, and your own renderer via three.js or the read primitives."
>
  <header class="docs-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Reference</p>
    <h1>One engine, many <span class="accent">surfaces</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Fundamental’s core (<code>@fundamental-engine/core</code>) computes the field against plain data
      and imports <strong>zero DOM</strong> — it’s renderer-agnostic. A thin host binds it to a surface.
      The web adapter, native Swift and Kotlin ports, Rust headless core, and custom renderers are
      distinct implementations. Their APIs, scheduling, rendering, and accessibility support differ.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="callout">
    <b>Shared concepts do not mean identical implementations.</b> The ports implement related force
    behavior, but host scheduling, measurement, rendering, accessibility, and API coverage vary.
    The shared golden covers six canonical forces; see the generated <a href="/docs/api/parity">parity matrix</a>
    for the exact set and tolerance.
  </div>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2>Web — the primary surface</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@fundamental-engine/dom</code> binds the engine to the page through <code>browserHost()</code>
      and the six registries (measurement, state, feedback, relationships, visual bindings, overlays):
      every <code>[data-body]</code> element becomes a body, and the field returns as <code>--field-*</code>
      CSS variables you style with. The authoring surfaces — <a href="/docs/guides/web-component">the
      <code>&lt;field-root&gt;</code> web component</a>, <a href="/docs/guides/react">React</a>,
      <a href="/docs/guides/typescript">vanilla / TypeScript</a> — all wrap it. This is the surface the whole site runs on.
    </p>
    <p><b>Get it:</b> <code>npm i @fundamental-engine/vanilla</code> (the recommended door). The listed API surface is protected against silent removal; additions and experimental surfaces may evolve in 0.x. See <a href="/docs/api/stability">API stability</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2>Native — Swift &amp; Kotlin, and the Field Lab</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>Swift port</strong> runs on Apple platforms (macOS / iOS / visionOS) with native
      rendering paths. Visual output and input/accessibility behavior belong to the host and are not
      covered by the shared force golden. Its showcase is the <strong>Field Lab</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <strong>Kotlin port</strong> implements the field on Android and is held to the same <strong>cross-plane conformance gate</strong>
      (one shared golden, JS / Swift / Kotlin), and the <code>FieldHandle</code> public API (relationship
      edges included). It ships a <code>:fundamental-platform</code> module (the six-phase
      discover→read→compute→state→write→render scheduler + the six registries), a
      <strong>Jetpack Compose</strong> host (<code>FieldView</code> + <code>Modifier.fieldBody()</code>),
      and a non-Compose <code>View</code>/<code>Canvas</code> host for imperative apps. It has its own
      desktop <strong>Field Lab</strong>. Source: the repo’s
      <a href="https://github.com/zachshallbetter/fundamental-engine/blob/main/android/README.md"><code>android/</code></a>
      directory.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <b>Parity — broad, not complete, and still preview.</b> Both are ports, and the web plane moved
      first historically. The big catch-up landed in June 2026 (#822 / #823 / #824) and the shared
      physics-conformance golden is green across planes — the field channels
      (<code>addField</code> / <code>sampleField</code>), the core force set and the interactive scenes
      are all there. But the handle is <strong>not</strong> at full parity, and the divergence runs both
      ways: of the {handleDim.union.length} callable members any plane declares, JS has
      <strong>{handleDim.counts.js}</strong>, Swift <strong>{handleDim.counts.swift}</strong> and Kotlin
      <strong>{handleDim.counts.kotlin}</strong> — the ports carry loop-control methods the web does not
      expose, and the web carries the focus/attention substrate the ports have not taken. (These numbers
      are generated from the port sources, not typed here.) API parity is not production-readiness
      either — treat the native plane as a fast-moving preview (see Status below). For the
      member-by-member breakdown see the <a href="/docs/api/parity">parity matrix</a> and the
      <a href="/docs/api/imperative">imperative API reference</a>.
    </div>
    <p>
      <b>Get it:</b> build it from source —
      <code>swift run FieldLab</code> in <code>swift/</code> (a packaging script,
      <code>swift/Scripts/release-fieldlab.sh</code>, produces the app bundle). Status: <b>preview.</b>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2>Your renderer — bring your own draw</h2>
    <p>
      <code>@fundamental-engine/three</code> runs the engine headless and renders its swarm as a
      <code>THREE.Points</code> layer in <em>your</em> WebGL scene (<a href="/docs/guides/three">the
      Three.js guide</a>). More generally, the engine exposes renderer-agnostic <strong>read
      primitives</strong> — <code>sample</code>, <code>sampleScalar</code>, <code>readParticles</code>,
      and the <a href="/docs/field-channels">field channels</a> (<code>addField</code>) — so you can bind
      the field to anything that draws, on your own loop. <a href="https://github.com/zachshallbetter/habitat">Habitat</a>
      (a simulation garden) is this surface in production.
    </p>
    <p><b>Get it:</b> <code>npm i @fundamental-engine/three three</code>. This adapter renders particles as a Three.js layer; your scene remains responsible for semantic and accessibility behavior.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2>At a glance</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Host / binding</th><th>Get it</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>Browser engines</b></td><td>Evergreen Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit are supported; legacy Internet Explorer is not.</td><td>Use the web packages</td><td>DOM APIs and graphics limits vary by browser/device</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Web</b></td><td><code>@fundamental-engine/dom</code> measures DOM geometry and can write CSS feedback; scheduling and visual output are web-specific.</td><td><code>npm i @fundamental-engine/vanilla</code></td><td>Primary integration; guarantees vary by symbol</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Native — Apple</b></td><td>Swift port; native host supplies measurement, input, rendering, and accessibility behavior.</td><td>Build from source in <code>swift/</code></td><td>Preview</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Native — Android</b></td><td>Kotlin port; Compose and View/Canvas hosts supply platform UI behavior.</td><td>Build from source (<a href="https://github.com/zachshallbetter/fundamental-engine/blob/main/android/README.md">android/</a>)</td><td>Preview</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Headless — Rust</b></td><td><code>fundamental-core</code> is experimental, implements 36 of 37 forces, and has no DOM or published crate.</td><td>Build from source (<a href="https://github.com/zachshallbetter/fundamental-engine/blob/main/rust/README.md">rust/</a>)</td><td>Experimental; no <code>relief</code> channel</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>Your renderer</b></td><td><code>@fundamental-engine/three</code> renders particles as <code>THREE.Points</code>; it does not provide DOM semantics or accessibility for your scene.</td><td><code>npm i @fundamental-engine/three three</code></td><td>WebGL adapter</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p class="aside">The shared conformance result covers six forces and their defined probes within the published tolerance; it does not certify visual parity, performance, accessibility, or every API method.</p>
</DocsLayout>
